      "writtenOn": "2026-09-15T10:07:50Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I don\u0027t understand the series: the previous change removed default fallback from Prolog into the SubmitRuleEvaluator class thus preparing it to be a central place to evaluate submit type. Now we have a new class SubmitTypeEvaluator which is the central place and it directly refers to the prolog machinery, bypassing the SubmitRuleEvaluator.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 56,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 58,
        "endChar": 80
      },
      "revId": "2c41acf7bb2bcfc5533add619059aacbef730942",
      "serverId": "173816e5-2b9a-37c3-8a2e-48639d4f1153"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "28d086b2_313dfe0f",
        "filename": "java/com/google/gerrit/server/project/SubmitTypeEvaluator.java",
        "patchSetId": 8
      },
      "lineNbr": 58,
      "author": {
        "id": 1065256
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-09-16T09:13:56Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The parent change solves the issue that the prolog machinery was also used to set the default. This was fine while the hierarchy was still Prolog -\u003e Default. Now it is Prolog -\u003e Override -\u003e Default. If the prolog rule evaluation would still return the default as before, we wouldn\u0027t be able to determine in all cases whether some rule applied or the default is being used and thus whether to look at overrides or not.\nRegarding the new class, it was introduced here, because the old class was dealing only with Prolog, which is now not the case anymore for submit types. Thus, the scope changed and I deemed it necessary to have a dedicated class for that. The parent change did not have the issue yet, thus I left the old class structure unchanged there (although it was admittedly not perfect from the start).",
      "parentUuid": "b939358c_5f76c14a",
